  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "The authors developed CAROLINA, a deep causal inference framework for discrete choice modeling that isolates causal effects from observed and unobserved confounders. Tested across synthetic data, virtual reality pedestrian experiments, and London revealed preference travel data, the model incorporates normalizing flows for counterfactual forecasting. Counterfactual evaluations indicate that interventions reducing pedestrian stress boost short crossing wait times by 35.35%, while reducing non-mandatory travel distances in London increases active and public transit adoption by 47%.",
      "why": "It enables transportation analysts to evaluate policy interventions using observational travel data without confounding correlations inflating or distorting predicted behavioral changes."
    }
  },
